Insulin is an anabolic hormone because it promotes the conversion of small molecules in the blood (like glucose) into larger storage molecules inside cells, such as glycogen (via glycogenesis) and fats (via lipogenesis), while also stimulating protein synthesis across many tissues. It does this by enhancing glucose uptake into liver, fat, and skeletal muscle cells and inhibiting liver glucose production, thereby lowering blood sugar levels. It is thus an anabolic hormone, promoting the conversion of small molecules in the blood into large molecules in the cells.[1]{c1}

When blood insulin is low, the opposite effect occurs: the body shifts toward widespread catabolism, particularly breaking down reserve body fat. Low insulin in the blood has the opposite effect, promoting widespread catabolism, especially of reserve body fat.[1]{c2}

