Photosynthetic organisms store the energy they capture as chemical energy within the bonds of intracellular organic compounds—complex molecules containing carbon—most typically carbohydrates such as sugars (mainly glucose, fructose and sucrose), starches, phytoglycogen and cellulose.[1]{c1}

When the organism needs to draw on this stored energy, its cells metabolize those organic compounds through cellular respiration, making the energy available to fuel metabolism.[1]{c2}

